Limitations of Ollama
1. Hardware & Performance

CPU-only is slow → On t3.large you can run models like Code Llama 7B, but generation will be slower compared to GPUs.

Memory limits → Larger models (13B, 34B) need more RAM (16 GB+ just to load). Your t3.large (8 GB) can’t handle them.

No GPU acceleration → Unless you run Ollama on a GPU-enabled instance (e.g., g4dn, p3, g5), it defaults to CPU.

2. Model Size & Disk Space

Models are large (3–10+ GB each). You had to resize EBS to 32 GB already. Running multiple models will require even more storage.

No automatic cleanup → You must manually manage models in /usr/share/ollama/.ollama/models.

3. Ecosystem Limitations

Not a training framework → Ollama is inference-only. You can’t fine-tune or retrain models with it (unlike Hugging Face + PyTorch).

Limited models → Only supports models converted to Ollama’s format (Llama, Code Llama, Mistral, Gemma, etc.).

No multi-modal (yet) → Ollama handles text-only. You can’t directly process images, audio, or video.

4. Networking / API

API is local-only by default (127.0.0.1:11434). You can expose it, but it’s not cloud-ready out of the box.

No built-in multi-user or scaling → It’s single-node; you need to wrap it in Docker/Kubernetes for clustering.

5. Compared to Cloud AI (like OpenAI, Vertex, Bedrock)

Pros:

Private (runs entirely on your machine).

Free after setup (no API bills).

Fast for local prototyping.

Cons:

Smaller models than cloud-hosted LLMs.

Missing advanced features: RAG connectors, function calling, embeddings, vector DB integrations.

Slower inference on non-GPU EC2.

 Where Ollama Works Best

Private/local AI coding assistant (like your script generator demo).

Testing LLMs without cloud dependency.

Educational demos (showing how models run locally).

Integrations → you can wrap Ollama with Python (like we did with ai_shell_gen.py) and build local apps.
